{"id":"nsv-machine-gun","slug":"nsv-machine-gun","name":"NSV","aliases":["Utyos-M","NSV-12.7","KT-12.7 gun","NSV .50 cal",".50 cal MG NSV","50 Cal. MG NSV","NSV (machine gun)","NSV heavy machine gun","NSV machine gun"],"kind":"firearm","category":"machine-guns","subcategory":"Heavy machine gun","description":"The NSV Utyos, is a Soviet heavy machine gun chambered in 12.7×108mm. It is named after the designers, G. I. Nikitin, Y. М. Sokolov and V. I. Volkov. It was designed to replace the DShK machine gun and was adopted by the Soviet Army in 1971.","history":"The Soviet Army began looking for a new heavy machine gun to replace its older SGM and DShK machine guns in the early 1950s. The Soviet Army liked the idea behind the German MG 42: a versatile weapon used on a variety of mounts to perform many different roles. Two Soviet weapon designers were asked to design one weapon each utilizing the same principle. Mikhail Kalashnikov's submission was approved following trials as it was found to be more reliable and cheaper to manufacture than the design of Grigory Nikitin and Yuri Sokolov. Kalashnikov's machine gun became the new standard machine gun, and was named PK.\nNikitin's and Sokolov's design was, however, not forgotten. It was eventually developed into the NSV heavy machine gun about 10 years later and selected in 1969 as the successor to the DShK and DShKM machine guns. It was accepted in service by the Soviet Army in 1971.\nFollowing the break-up of the Soviet Union, Russia began developing the Kord heavy machine gun to replace the NSV \"Utyos\" and which entered service in 1998. The Metallist factory in what is now Oral, Kazakhstan struggled to find customers in the 1990s. The factory was converted to focus on industrial production in the oil and gas sector in 2003.","facts":[],"specifications":[{"group":"Cartridge & Action","items":[{"label":"Cartridge","value":"12.7×108mm"},{"label":"Cartridge","value":"12.7×99mm NATO"},{"label":"Cartridge","value":"(For Polish WKM-B)"},{"label":"Action","value":"Gas-operated"},{"label":"Rate of Fire","value":"700–800 rounds/min"},{"label":"Muzzle Velocity","value":"845 m/s (2,772 ft/s)","numeric":845,"unit":"m/s"},{"label":"Feed System","value":"50-round belt"},{"label":"Sights","value":"Iron sights"}]},{"group":"Dimensions","items":[{"label":"Weight","value":"25 kg (gun only)","numeric":25,"unit":"kg"},{"label":"Weight","value":"41 kg on tripod","numeric":41,"unit":"kg"},{"label":"Weight","value":"11 kg (50-round belt)","numeric":11,"unit":"kg"},{"label":"Length","value":"1560 mm","numeric":1560,"unit":"mm"}]},{"group":"Performance","items":[{"label":"Effective Range","value":"1500 m vs. air (maximum)","numeric":1500,"unit":"m"},{"label":"Effective Range","value":"2000 m vs. ground targets","numeric":2000,"unit":"m"}]},{"group":"Production","items":[{"label":"Designer","value":"G.I. Nikitin, Y.М. Sokolov, V.I. Volkov"},{"label":"Designed","value":"Central Design Bureau of Sports and Hunting Weaponry, Tula, 1969"},{"label":"Manufacturer","value":"West-Kazakhstan machine building company JSC (ZKMK)"},{"label":"Produced","value":"1971–present"}]},{"group":"Service","items":[{"label":"Origin","value":"Soviet Union"},{"label":"In Service","value":"1971–present"}]}],"specIndex":{"Cartridge":"12.7×108mm","Action":"Gas-operated","Rate of Fire":"700–800 rounds/min","Muzzle Velocity":"845 m/s (2,772 ft/s)","Feed System":"50-round belt","Sights":"Iron sights","Weight":"25 kg (gun only)","Length":"1560 mm","Effective Range":"1500 m vs. air (maximum)","Designer":"G.I. Nikitin, Y.М. Sokolov, V.I. Volkov","Designed":"Central Design Bureau of Sports and Hunting Weaponry, Tula, 1969","Manufacturer":"West-Kazakhstan machine building company JSC (ZKMK)","Produced":"1971–present","Origin":"Soviet Union","In Service":"1971–present"},"images":{"hero":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/90/NSV_machine_gun-04.jpg","descriptionUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NSV_machine_gun-